  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 326 AM CST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\nCurrent 500mb analysis reveals that the primary axis of the mid-\nlevel trough has moved east of our forecast area as high pressure \ncontinues to move in behind it. The surface low continues to move \neast into SE Ontario bringing the trailing cold front south of our \nforecast area as high pressure builds in from the north over the \nNorthern Plains. The overall synoptic setup supports northerly flow \nand CAA into the region which will result in slightly cooler \ntemperatures today with highs in the low to mid 50s. Low level \nclouds are going to stick around for most of today and tonight as \nforecast soundings depict a lingering saturated layer near the \nsurface with an inversion aloft. Winds at the surface will also be \nweak or non-existent due to the weak pressure gradient across the \nforecast area, which will make it difficult for that saturated layer \nto mix out. Deep lift within the saturated layer will also support \npatchy drizzle through at least the morning hours, potentially into \nthis evening as it tapers off from NE to SW. \n\nOvernight, the 500mb ridge and surface high will settle into our \nregion, supporting continued CAA and colder temperatures. However, \nhow low temperatures drop will depend on how far low level clouds \nretreat overnight. Current guidance keep clouds around for areas \nwest of highway 65, which will limit radiational cooling and keep \nlows at or above 32 degrees. Clouds are more likely to dissipate \neast of 65 allowing for more efficient radiational cooling and a 60-\n90% chance of dropping below freezing. Therefore, we have issued a \nfreeze warning for counties along and east of highway 65 and a frost \nadvisory west of 65. \n\nAs we move into late this weekend and early next week, the upper-\nlevel ridge will deamplify across the northern CONUS resulting in \nquasi-zonal flow while high pressure at the surface will move east, \nsupporting more southwesterly flow into the forecast area. This will \ndrive highs back into the mid to upper 60s and lows into the mid to \nupper 40s and conditions should remain dry for the beginning of the \nwork week.",
